const e=[{id:"sv-2-1841",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

In the Measure phase, the book advises against trying to make the process [?] before working on it.`,options:["capable","centred","stable","standardised"],answer:2,hint:"Recall the book’s warning about what NOT to do to a process at the start of Measure.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book (p. 217): do not try to make your process **stable** before working on it — it is a project because something is wrong with it."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Capable, centred and standardised describe later improvement or control outcomes, not the Measure warning."}],final:"Do not stabilise before working on it → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — making the process capable is the goal of Improve, not something done before the baseline.","Incorrect — centring the mean is an Improve action; the book’s warning is about stability.","","Incorrect — standardising work is a Control-phase activity, not the subject of this warning."],bookRef:"Book p. 217"},{id:"sv-2-1842",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

Capability studies are most meaningful when they are performed on [?] processes.`,options:["automated","capable","centred","stable"],answer:3,hint:"A capability index predicts the future only if the process behaves the same way over time.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book (p. 211): capability studies are most meaningful on **stable** processes — only then does today’s distribution predict tomorrow’s."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"“Capable” is the study’s conclusion, “centred” only changes Cpk versus Cp, and automation is irrelevant."}],final:"Capability is most meaningful on stable processes → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — automation has nothing to do with whether a capability index can predict future output.","Incorrect — circular: whether the process is capable is what the study is trying to find out.","Incorrect — an off-centre process still gets a meaningful Cpk; centring affects the result, not its validity.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 211"},{id:"sv-2-1843",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:2,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

[?] variation is the variation left over after special cause variation has been removed.`,options:["Assignable","Common cause","Measurement","Unnatural"],answer:1,hint:"Which kind of variation is still there when every assignable cause is gone?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book (p. 158) defines **common cause** variation as the variation left over after special cause variation has been removed — the natural noise of the process."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Assignable and unnatural are synonyms for special cause; measurement variation belongs to MSA."}],final:"Leftover natural noise = common cause → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — “assignable cause” is another name for special cause, the variation that has been removed.","","Incorrect — measurement variation comes from the gauge system and is studied in MSA, not defined this way.","Incorrect — “unnatural variability” is the book’s other name for special cause variation."],bookRef:"Book p. 158"},{id:"sv-2-1844",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

Short-term data gives a snapshot of the process, whereas long-term data gives a [?].`,options:["census","forecast","histogram","video"],answer:3,hint:"The book contrasts a single still picture with a moving picture over time.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s short-term vs long-term table: short-term data is a **snapshot**, long-term data is a **video** — it shows performance unfolding over time."},{title:"Apply",body:"A video captures shifts, drifts and changes of people, materials and shifts that a snapshot misses."}],final:"Short-term = snapshot, long-term = video → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a census means measuring every unit; long-term is about time span, not completeness.","Incorrect — long-term data records what has happened; it is not a forecast.","Incorrect — a histogram is a graph type and ignores time order entirely.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 212–213"},{id:"sv-2-1845",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"statements",difficulty:2,stem:`Which statements about stability in the Measure phase are CORRECT?

1. There is no reason to assume the process is stable at this point.
2. A baseline capability study “draws a line in the sand” for later comparison.
3. The baseline should be postponed until every special cause has been removed.
4. If the process is unstable, the capability study should be skipped.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:0,hint:"Recall what the book says about assuming stability, and what the Measure baseline is for.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book (pp. 215–217): there is **no reason to assume stability** in Measure; a capability study simply **draws a line in the sand**; do **not** try to make the process stable first."},{title:"Apply",body:"Statements 1 and 2 are true. Statements 3 and 4 delay or skip the baseline, which the book rejects."}],final:"Statements 1 and 2 are correct → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — statement 3 is false: the book says not to make the process stable before working on it.","Incorrect — both are false: removing special causes and skipping the study contradict the book’s Measure advice.","Incorrect — statement 4 is false: an unstable process still gets a baseline, with the instability acknowledged."],bookRef:"Book pp. 215–217"},{id:"sv-2-1846",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"statements",difficulty:2,stem:`Which statements about common cause and special cause variation are CORRECT?

1. Special causes can be ignored because they are rare.
2. Common cause variation is the natural, random noise of a process.
3. A special cause is an assignable cause that can be found and removed.
4. Adjusting the process after every common-cause point reduces variation.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:1,hint:"Separate natural noise from findable causes, then recall what over-adjustment does.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Common cause = natural, random, many unknown sources (p. 158). Special cause = assignable, findable and removable (p. 523)."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Statement 1 ignores the opportunity special causes offer; statement 4 describes tampering, which makes variation worse (p. 544)."}],final:"Statements 2 and 3 are correct → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — statement 1 is false: the book calls special causes an opportunity to investigate.","","Incorrect — statement 4 is false: reacting to common cause is tampering and increases variation.","Incorrect — statements 1 and 4 are both false (ignoring special causes; tampering)."],bookRef:"Book pp. 158, 523, 544"},{id:"sv-2-1847",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"statements",difficulty:3,stem:`Which statements about short-term and long-term data are CORRECT?

1. Long-term data are potentially free of special causes.
2. Long-term variation is usually smaller than short-term variation.
3. Short-term data shows the optimal, or entitlement, performance of the process.
4. Data collected over a month or more is generally treated as long-term.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:2,hint:"Short-term = narrow snapshot; long-term = everything that happens over time.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book (pp. 212–214): short-term data are potentially free of special causes and show entitlement; long-term data include common and special causes, and long-term variation is larger."},{title:"Apply",body:"A month or more is roughly long-term; two weeks or less is short-term. Statements 3 and 4 are true."}],final:"Statements 3 and 4 are correct → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — both are false: long-term data include special causes and show more variation.","Incorrect — statement 2 is false: long-term variation is almost always larger than short-term.","","Incorrect — statement 1 is false: it is short-term data that is potentially free of special causes."],bookRef:"Book pp. 212–214"},{id:"sv-2-1848",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:3,stem:"Which is NOT a source of extra variation that usually appears in long-term data but may be missing from short-term data?",options:["Changes between material lots and suppliers","Different operators and shifts","Temperature changes and tool wear over time","The fixed resolution of the gauge used for every reading"],answer:3,hint:"Which option is the same in a one-hour sample and a six-month sample?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book (p. 213): long-term variation is larger because materials, temperature, people, suppliers and wear all add variation over time."},{title:"Apply",body:"A gauge’s fixed resolution affects every reading equally, short- or long-term; it is not an extra long-term source."}],final:"Fixed gauge resolution is not a long-term source → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — lot and supplier changes are a classic long-term source of variation.","Incorrect — people and shifts change over a long period and add variation.","Incorrect — temperature and wear build up over time and widen long-term spread.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 212–214"},{id:"sv-2-1849",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"Which is NOT a characteristic of short-term data, according to the book?",options:["It covers a narrow inference space, such as one shift or one batch","It is potentially free of special causes","It shows the optimal, or entitlement, performance of the process","It includes both common and special cause variation"],answer:3,hint:"Which of these belongs to the long-term column of the book’s table?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book table (p. 212): short-term — narrow inference space, potentially free of special causes, shows optimal performance. Long-term — broad, includes common **and** special causes."},{title:"Apply",body:"Including both kinds of cause is the long-term description, so it is the odd one out."}],final:"Common + special causes describes long-term data → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a narrow inference space is exactly what makes data short-term.","Incorrect — the book says short-term data is potentially free of special causes.","Incorrect — short-term data shows the best (entitlement) performance the process can achieve.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 212"},{id:"sv-2-1850",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:1,stem:"In an injection-moulding process, which is NOT a likely source of special-cause variation?",options:["A heater band failing part-way through a shift","A resin lot from a new, unqualified supplier","A mould changeover carried out without the standard procedure","The normal minute-to-minute fluctuation of barrel temperature within its usual band"],answer:3,hint:"Special causes are specific, findable events; common causes are the everyday background noise.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Special (assignable) causes are known, findable factors; common causes are many small unknown factors producing random noise (pp. 158, 523)."},{title:"Apply",body:"Normal fluctuation inside the usual band is background noise — common cause. The other three are specific events."}],final:"Normal fluctuation within the usual band = common cause → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a failed heater band is a specific, assignable event: a special cause.","Incorrect — a new, unqualified material lot is a classic assignable (special) cause.","Incorrect — a non-standard changeover is a findable event that changes the output: a special cause.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 158, 523"},{id:"sv-2-1851",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"A special cause is found in the Measure baseline. Which response is NOT appropriate?",options:["Recording what happened at that time and why","Investigating the assignable cause behind the signal","Considering whether it can be removed or built into the process","Treating it as normal process behaviour in the baseline description"],answer:3,hint:"A special cause is not part of how the process normally behaves.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Special causes must be acknowledged (p. 231): record them, investigate them, and decide whether to remove them (bad) or build them in (good)."},{title:"Apply",body:"Describing the signal as normal behaviour hides it inside the baseline, which is the inappropriate response."}],final:"Treating a special cause as normal behaviour is inappropriate → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — recording the event is exactly what the book’s outage example recommends.","Incorrect — a special cause should be investigated; it is an improvement opportunity.","Incorrect — the Measure checklist asks whether bad signals can be removed and good ones built in.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 215, 231"},{id:"sv-2-1852",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A plant produces **100,000 connectors in a single day**, and the team uses all of them as its capability data. How should this data set be classified?",options:["Long-term, because the sample is far larger than 100 points","Short-term, because it covers only one day of operation","Long-term, because it includes every unit made in the period","Neither, because classification depends on the specification width"],answer:1,hint:"The book warns: do not be misled by volume.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book (p. 214): 100,000 units made in one day is still **short-term**; 20 units a day for 3 months is long-term."},{title:"Apply",body:"One day covers one narrow set of conditions, however many units were made."}],final:"One day, any volume = short-term → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — volume does not make data long-term; the book judges by the time span and sources covered.","","Incorrect — measuring every unit of one day is still one day; it misses shifts, batches and drift.","Incorrect — short- or long-term depends on time span and inference space, not on the specification."],trap:"Large sample size is not the same as long-term data.",bookRef:"Book p. 214"},{id:"sv-2-1853",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A Green Belt records **40 consecutive invoices** processed by one experienced clerk during one morning. What does this data set mainly show?",options:["The real performance customers experience over time","The best performance the process can achieve, its entitlement","The long-term baseline for the project charter","The effect of special causes across shifts and suppliers"],answer:1,hint:"Narrow inference space, potentially free of special causes — which kind of data is that?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Short-term data (p. 212): narrow inference space, potentially free of special causes, shows the **optimal (entitlement)** performance."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Customer experience, baseline and cross-shift effects all need long-term data."}],final:"Short-term snapshot shows entitlement → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — customer experience over time needs long-term data covering many conditions.","","Incorrect — the book says a baseline always means long-term performance.","Incorrect — one clerk, one morning cannot show variation across shifts and suppliers."],bookRef:"Book p. 212"},{id:"sv-2-1854",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"After a new call script is introduced, the I chart of average handling time (lower is better) shows **9 days in a row below the centre line**. How should the team interpret this?",options:["Common cause variation that needs no attention","A random run that will disappear as more data arrive","Evidence that the control limits were calculated wrongly","A special-cause shift that is a good signal to build into the process"],answer:3,hint:"Use the exam run rule for a shift, then ask whether the shift helps or hurts.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Exam rule: **9 points in a row** on one side of the centre line = a shift (special cause)."},{title:"Apply",body:"Handling time fell after the script change, so this is a **good signal** — the Measure checklist asks whether good signals can be built into the process (p. 231)."}],final:"Shift in the desired direction = good signal to keep → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — 9 in a row on one side is a non-random pattern, not common cause noise.","Incorrect — a run of 9 is very unlikely by chance; it signals a real shift.","Incorrect — the run reflects a change in the process, not an error in the limits.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 231, 532–533"},{id:"sv-2-1855",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A team receives 200 machining cycle times **sorted from smallest to largest**, with no timestamps or sequence numbers. What prevents it from assessing stability?",options:["The sample size is too small","The data may not be normally distributed","The time order of the data has been lost","The specification limits have not been supplied"],answer:2,hint:"Stability means consistency over time. What does a stability check need to see?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Stability is assessed with a time series plot or control chart (p. 215), which needs the data in **production order**."},{title:"Apply",body:"Sorted data destroys the sequence, so shifts, trends and cycles cannot be seen."}],final:"No time order = no stability check → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — 200 points is plenty; the problem is that their order is unknown.","Incorrect — normality matters for capability indices, not for judging stability over time.","","Incorrect — specification limits are needed for capability, not for stability."],bookRef:"Book p. 215"},{id:"sv-2-1856",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A supervisor adds the 4-hour SLA to an I chart of ticket resolution time and labels it the “upper control limit”. What is wrong with this?",options:["Control limits for service processes should be set at ±2σ instead","Control limits must be calculated from the process data, not taken from the customer requirement","The SLA should be drawn as the centre line instead","Specification limits belong on the MR chart rather than the I chart"],answer:1,hint:"Control limits are the voice of the process; the SLA is the voice of the customer.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Control limits are calculated from the process data at about ±3σ (pp. 525–527). The book says: **never draw specification limits on a control chart** (p. 524)."},{title:"Apply",body:"Labelling the SLA as a control limit mixes stability (VOP) with capability (VOC)."}],final:"Control limits come from the data, never from the SLA → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — control limits are at ±3σ; the issue is their source, not the multiplier.","","Incorrect — the centre line is the process average, not a customer requirement.","Incorrect — specification limits belong on no control chart at all."],bookRef:"Book pp. 524–527"},{id:"sv-2-1857",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A team runs an Anderson-Darling test on 60 days of handling time (p = 0.45) and reports: “The data are normal, so the process is stable.” What is wrong with this conclusion?",options:["A p-value of 0.45 proves the data are not normal","A normality test ignores time order, so it cannot show whether the process is consistent over time","Stability can only be judged once the capability indices are known","Sixty days is too little data for any conclusion about the process"],answer:1,hint:"Normality describes shape; stability describes behaviour over time.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Stability is established with a time series plot or control chart (p. 215). A normality test looks at the shape of the pooled data and would give the same result if the points were shuffled."},{title:"Apply",body:"The team must chart the 60 days in order and apply the special-cause tests."}],final:"Normal ≠ stable → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — p > 0.05 means fail to reject normality; it does not prove non-normality.","","Incorrect — stability is checked before capability, not after it.","Incorrect — 60 days is a reasonable period; the problem is the tool, not the amount of data."],trap:"Shifted or trending data can still pass a normality test.",bookRef:"Book pp. 156, 215"},{id:"sv-2-1858",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A team has just accepted its measurement system (Gage R&R %Study Var = 8%). According to the book’s Measure roadmap, what should it do next?",options:["Calculate Cp and Cpk for the baseline","Start hypothesis testing on the potential X’s","Repeat the Gage R&R with more appraisers","Assess the stability of the process (statistical control)"],answer:3,hint:"Recall the order: measurement system → ? → capability.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book roadmap (p. 230): after the measurement system is accepted, **Assess Stability (Statistical Control)**, then **Assess Capability**."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Capability is next-but-one; Analyze tests come later; an 8% Gage R&R is already acceptable."}],final:"MSA → stability → capability → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — capability comes after stability in the roadmap.","Incorrect — hypothesis testing of X’s belongs to Analyze, after the baseline.","Incorrect — 8% is acceptable (<10%), so the MSA does not need repeating.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 230"},{id:"sv-2-1859",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A process engineer notices the same burst of scrap **every Monday morning** after the weekend shutdown. What kind of variation is this?",options:["Common cause, because it happens every week","Measurement variation, because it occurs at start-up","Special cause, because it is a non-random pattern with an assignable source","Tampering, because the line was stopped"],answer:2,hint:"Recurring is not the same as random. Can the cause be pointed to?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Special causes produce **non-random** patterns (shifts, cycles, outliers) and have an **assignable** source (p. 523)."},{title:"Apply",body:"A Monday-only burst linked to restart is a cycle with a known trigger — special cause, even though it recurs."}],final:"Recurring, assignable pattern = special cause → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — repeating regularly does not make it common cause; it is a findable, non-random pattern.","Incorrect — nothing suggests the gauge is at fault; the product itself is scrapped.","","Incorrect — tampering is adjusting a stable process for noise; a shutdown is not that."],trap:"A cause that repeats regularly is still special if it is assignable and non-random.",bookRef:"Book pp. 158, 523"},{id:"sv-2-1860",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A supplier’s capability study used one week of data from one shift. The customer’s data from the past six months show a much wider spread for the same part. Which explanation is MOST likely?",options:["Long-term data include extra sources such as shifts, material lots and people, so variation is larger","The supplier’s short-term study must contain a measurement error","The customer’s six months of data must contain only common cause variation","Short-term variation is normally larger than long-term variation"],answer:0,hint:"What extra sources of variation appear when data cover months instead of one shift?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book (p. 213): long-term variation is always larger — materials, temperature, people, suppliers and wear all add variation; even stable processes drift about 1.5 SD."},{title:"Apply",body:"One week, one shift is a snapshot; six months is a video with all those sources included."}],final:"Long-term spread is wider because more sources act → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — no evidence of a gauge problem; a narrower short-term spread is expected.","Incorrect — long-term data include common and special causes.","Incorrect — the reverse is true: long-term variation is almost always larger."],bookRef:"Book pp. 212–214"},{id:"sv-2-1861",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:"In Measure, a team finds three special-cause days in its baseline (an outage, a strike and a data-feed failure). The Green Belt **deletes the three days** and reports the capability without mentioning them. Was this an appropriate action?",options:["Yes, because outliers distort capability indices","Yes, because a baseline should show only common cause variation","No, because special causes should be acknowledged and studied as improvement opportunities","No, because the three days should be replaced by the average value"],answer:2,hint:"What does the Measure checklist ask the team to do with special causes?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Measure checklist (p. 231): if the process is not stable, have the special causes been **acknowledged**? The book also calls special causes an **opportunity**."},{title:"Apply",body:"Silently deleting the days hides both the instability and the opportunity."}],final:"Acknowledge, do not hide, special causes → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — hiding real events misrepresents the baseline; the book asks for special causes to be acknowledged.","Incorrect — the baseline is the process as it is; there is no reason to assume stability in Measure.","","Incorrect — replacing data with an average is still hiding the special causes."],bookRef:"Book pp. 215, 231"},{id:"sv-2-1862",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:2,stem:"An Xbar-R chart of fill weight shows points beyond the limits on **both** the Xbar chart and the R chart. What should the team do FIRST?",options:["Investigate the Xbar signals, because the mean matters most to customers","Investigate the R chart signals, because the Xbar limits are calculated from the average range","Recalculate both sets of limits without the flagged points","Compare the Xbar points with the specification limits"],answer:1,hint:"Which chart’s limits depend on the other chart?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Exam convention: read the **R (or MR) chart first**. The Xbar limits use R-bar, so an unstable R chart makes the Xbar limits meaningless."},{title:"Apply",body:"Investigate the range signals first, then interpret the Xbar chart."}],final:"Read the R chart first → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — if the range is unstable, the Xbar limits themselves are unreliable, so the R chart comes first.","","Incorrect — removing points before finding their causes hides special causes.","Incorrect — specification limits are never used to judge stability on a control chart."],bookRef:"Book pp. 525–533"},{id:"sv-2-1863",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:2,stem:"A fill-weight process shows no control-chart signals. Each time a bottle is weighed slightly below the centre line, the operator adjusts the filler to compensate. Was this an appropriate action?",options:["Yes, because each adjustment re-centres the process","Yes, because light bottles are special causes","No, because adjustments should only be made once per shift","No, because reacting to common cause variation is tampering and increases variation"],answer:3,hint:"What happens when a stable process is adjusted for every random point?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Adjusting a stable process in response to common cause variation is **tampering**, which increases variation (p. 544)."},{title:"Apply",body:"No signals = common cause only; the right response is to leave it alone or improve the system if the spread is too wide."}],final:"Reacting to noise = tampering → more variation → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — adjusting for random noise pushes the process off centre more often, not less.","Incorrect — points inside the limits with no pattern are common cause, not special cause.","Incorrect — the frequency is not the issue; any adjustment for common cause noise is tampering.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 544"},{id:"sv-2-1864",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:"During Measure, the Green Belt sees that the I chart of seal strength steps up each time a new batch of raw film arrives. What is the BEST next step?",options:["Wait until the batches stop changing before measuring the baseline","Record the signal as a special cause and carry the film batch forward as a likely X to investigate","Widen the control limits so that the batch changes no longer show as signals","Treat the batch changes as common cause because new batches arrive every week"],answer:1,hint:"The Measure checklist asks the team to acknowledge special causes. What are they good for?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Special causes must be acknowledged (p. 231) and are an opportunity — they point to findable causes."},{title:"Apply",body:"A batch-linked step is an assignable cause; record it and investigate film batch as an X."}],final:"Acknowledge the signal → investigate as an X → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the book says not to make the process stable before working on it.","","Incorrect — limits come from the data; widening them hides a real signal.","Incorrect — regular arrival does not make it common cause; the steps are non-random and assignable."],bookRef:"Book pp. 217, 231"},{id:"sv-2-1865",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:"A time series of handling time shows one tight three-week period when an experienced team was on duty. What is the BEST next step?",options:["Use that period as the official project baseline","Remove that period from the data as an outlier","Calculate the control limits from that period only","Find out which X’s were different in that period so the performance can be repeated"],answer:3,hint:"The tightest stretch on a time series shows what the process is entitled to.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book (p. 215): the region of tightest variation shows the process **entitlement**; if we find the X’s causing the difference we should be able to perform like that again."},{title:"Apply",body:"So investigate what was different (people, method) rather than relabelling or deleting the period."}],final:"Entitlement → find the X’s to repeat it → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the baseline is long-term performance; a tight period is entitlement, not the baseline.","Incorrect — the tight period is valuable evidence of what the process can do, not an error.","Incorrect — limits from the best period would flag normal behaviour as special cause.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 215"},{id:"sv-2-1866",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`An operator has been re-adjusting a filler after every check. The quality engineer plots the last 30 hourly averages:

\`\`\`
Run Chart of Hourly Fill Weight (g)

Number of runs about median:        16    Number of runs up or down:        27
Expected number of runs:          16.0    Expected number of runs:        19.7
Longest run about median:            3    Longest run up or down:            2
Approx P-Value for Clustering:   0.500    Approx P-Value for Trends:     0.998
Approx P-Value for Mixtures:     0.500    Approx P-Value for Oscillation: 0.002
\`\`\`

Which pattern does the output confirm at α = 0.05?`,options:["Oscillation: the values alternate up and down more often than chance would explain","Clustering: groups of points sit far from the median","A trend: the values drift steadily in one direction","No pattern: only common cause variation is present"],answer:0,hint:"Find the only p-value below α and name its pattern.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"Oscillation p = 0.002 < 0.05; runs up or down (27) exceed the 19.7 expected. The other p-values are large."},{title:"Interpret",body:"Rapid up-and-down alternation is the typical fingerprint of over-adjustment — tampering, which adds variation (p. 544)."}],final:"Oscillation p < 0.05 → tampering fingerprint → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the clustering p-value is 0.500, far above 0.05.","Incorrect — the trends p-value is 0.998; there is no evidence of a trend.","Incorrect — the oscillation p-value of 0.002 is below 0.05, so a pattern is present."],bookRef:"Syllabus 2.4.2; Book p. 544"},{id:"sv-2-1867",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`\`\`\`
Test Results for I Chart of Machining Cycle Time

TEST 6. 4 out of 5 points more than 1 standard deviation from center line
        (on one side of CL).
Test Failed at points:  22, 23
\`\`\`

All points lie inside the control limits. What does this result indicate?`,options:["The process is stable, because no point is beyond the control limits","The process is not capable, because points are close to the specification limits","The process is not stable: a small sustained shift has been signalled even though no point is outside the limits","The measurement system has too little resolution to detect changes"],answer:2,hint:"Special-cause rules include patterns inside the limits, not only points beyond 3σ.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"Test 6 (4 of 5 points beyond 1σ on one side) failed at points 22 and 23."},{title:"Interpret",body:"It is one of the zone rules (book pp. 532–533) that detect a smaller shift in the mean — a special cause, so the process is not stable."}],final:"Test 6 failure = small shift → not stable → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — points inside the limits can still form a non-random pattern; Test 6 is a special-cause rule.","Incorrect — control charts say nothing about specification limits or capability.","","Incorrect — poor resolution shows as few distinct values, not a pattern of points beyond 1σ."],bookRef:"Book pp. 532–533"},{id:"sv-2-1868",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A team summarises a 60-day time series of daily ticket backlog:

\`\`\`
Time Series Summary - Daily Backlog (tickets)

Days     Mean    StDev   Points beyond control limits
1-20     30.2    2.1     0
21-40    36.4    2.0     4
41-60    30.0    2.2     0
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is BEST supported?`,options:["The process is stable, because the standard deviation hardly changed","The process is not stable: the mean shifted up for days 21-40, so the team should find out what changed then","The process is stable, because the mean returned to about 30 by day 41","The process is not capable, because four points were beyond the control limits"],answer:1,hint:"Stable means consistent over time — check both the mean and the spread.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"Days 21-40 have a mean of 36.4 versus about 30 elsewhere, with 4 points beyond the limits."},{title:"Interpret",body:"A temporary shift in level is a special cause: the process is not stable. The cause of the change on day 21 should be investigated."}],final:"Temporary mean shift = not stable → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a stable spread does not make up for a shift in the mean.","","Incorrect — a temporary shift is still a special cause; returning to normal does not cancel it.","Incorrect — control limits are not specification limits; capability is not assessed here."],bookRef:"Book pp. 215, 523"},{id:"sv-2-1869",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`Two data sets are available for the baseline of order-fulfilment time:

\`\`\`
Data set   Period covered        Shifts   Orders   StDev (h)
  P        1 day (peak sale)     3        5,200    1.1
  Q        3 months              all        180    2.4
\`\`\`

Which data set should be used as the project baseline, and why?`,options:["Q, because a baseline means long-term performance covering all sources of variation","P, because its much larger sample gives a more precise baseline","P, because its smaller standard deviation shows the true process","Q, because a baseline should always use fewer than 200 points"],answer:0,hint:"What does the book say a baseline always means?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book (p. 214): a **baseline** always means **long-term** performance; do not be misled by volume."},{title:"Read the table",body:"Q covers three months and all shifts (a video); P is one day (a snapshot), even with 5,200 orders."}],final:"Baseline = long-term → data set Q → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — volume is not duration; one day is short-term however many orders it contains.","Incorrect — the smaller spread reflects a narrow snapshot (entitlement), not real long-term performance.","Incorrect — the reason is the time span covered, not a rule about point counts."],bookRef:"Book pp. 212–214"},{id:"sv-2-1870",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`\`\`\`
Test Results for I Chart of Torque (Nm), two spindles sampled alternately

TEST 8. 8 points in a row more than 1 standard deviation from center line
        (above and below CL).
Test Failed at points:  17, 29, 30
\`\`\`

What does this result MOST likely indicate?`,options:["A trend caused by gradual tool wear","A mixture: the data come from two different streams, so points avoid the centre line","Points hugging the centre line because the limits are inflated","A single extreme value caused by one bad part"],answer:1,hint:"Points staying away from the centre on both sides suggests more than one population.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"Test 8: 8 points in a row beyond 1σ on either side — the points avoid the centre zone."},{title:"Interpret",body:"With two spindles sampled alternately, this mixture pattern suggests the spindles run at different levels — a special cause."}],final:"Test 8 = mixture of two streams → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a trend is detected by Test 3 (6 points steadily increasing or decreasing).","","Incorrect — points hugging the centre line is Test 7, the opposite pattern.","Incorrect — a single extreme point is Test 1 (one point beyond 3σ)."],bookRef:"Syllabus 2.4.2"},{id:"sv-2-1871",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`\`\`\`
Test Results for I Chart of Daily Avg Call Wait (s)

TEST 7. 15 points within 1 standard deviation of center line
        (above and below CL).
Test Failed at points:  41
\`\`\`

A supervisor says: “Excellent — the process is in perfect control.” Which response is BEST?`,options:["Agree, because points close to the centre line prove the process is in control","A trend has started that will soon cross the upper control limit","The process must now be capable, because the variation is small","The pattern is non-random and should be investigated, for example for stratified data or a real drop in variation"],answer:3,hint:"Too little variation for the limits is also a signal.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"Test 7 failed: 15 points in a row within 1σ of the centre line."},{title:"Interpret",body:"This hugging pattern is non-random. Typical causes are limits inflated by mixing streams in a subgroup, or a genuine reduction in variation that should be confirmed and built in."}],final:"Test 7 = non-random hugging → investigate → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — Test 7 flags the pattern as non-random; “too good to be true” still needs explaining.","Incorrect — a trend is Test 3 (6 in a row increasing or decreasing), not Test 7.","Incorrect — control charts do not show capability; that needs a comparison with specifications.",""],trap:"A chart that looks “too good” can still be signalling a special cause.",bookRef:"Syllabus 2.4.2"},{id:"sv-2-1872",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A team charts call-centre wait times for a stability check:

\`\`\`
I Chart of Wait Time (s)    Data: 08:00–12:00, Tuesday 14 May, n = 40

Test Results for I Chart of Wait Time:   No tests failed.
Test Results for MR Chart of Wait Time:  No tests failed.
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is BEST supported?`,options:["The process is stable over the long term, so the baseline capability can be calculated","The process is unstable, because 40 points are too few for a control chart","The process looked stable during that morning, but this short-term snapshot cannot show long-term stability","The process is capable, because no tests failed on either chart"],answer:2,hint:"Check the time span before generalising a stability result.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"No tests failed on either chart, but all 40 points come from one four-hour window."},{title:"Interpret",body:"That is short-term data — a snapshot (p. 212). Long-term stability needs data across days, shifts and conditions."}],final:"Stable snapshot ≠ long-term stability → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — one morning is a narrow inference space; it says nothing about other days, shifts or weeks.","Incorrect — 40 points is enough to chart; the issue is the time span, not instability.","","Incorrect — no failed tests means stable during that window, not capable."],bookRef:"Book pp. 212–215"},{id:"sv-2-1873",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"According to the book, a process must be made stable before a baseline capability study can be carried out in the Measure phase.",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"Recall the book’s advice on stability at the start of Measure.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book (p. 217): do **not** try to make your process stable before working on it. There is no reason to assume stability in Measure; the baseline simply draws a line in the sand (p. 215)."},{title:"Decide",body:"The statement contradicts the book, so it is false."}],final:"Baseline first, stability not required beforehand → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the book says do not try to make the process stable before working on it.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 215–217"},{id:"sv-2-1874",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`Order these special-cause rules from the **fewest** to the **most** points needed to trigger them (exam convention):

- **P** Points in a row on the same side of the centre line
- **Q** Points in a row alternating up and down
- **R** A point more than 3σ from the centre line
- **S** Points in a row steadily increasing or decreasing`,options:["R, P, S, Q","S, R, P, Q","R, S, Q, P","R, S, P, Q"],answer:3,hint:"Recall the number in each rule: 1, 6, 9 and 14.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Exam rules (book pp. 532–533): 1 point beyond 3σ (Test 1); 6 in a row trending (Test 3); 9 in a row on one side (Test 2); 14 alternating (Test 4)."},{title:"Apply",body:"Fewest to most: R (1), S (6), P (9), Q (14)."}],final:"1 → 6 → 9 → 14 = R, S, P, Q → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a trend needs 6 points, fewer than the 9 needed for a shift.","Incorrect — a single point beyond 3σ needs only one point, fewer than a 6-point trend.","Incorrect — alternation needs 14 points, more than the 9 for a shift.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 532–533"},{id:"sv-2-1875",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`Put these steps for checking process stability in order:

- **P** Apply the tests for special causes
- **Q** Collect the data in time order
- **R** Investigate any assignable cause behind a signal
- **S** Plot the data with a centre line and control limits calculated from the data`,options:["S, Q, P, R","Q, S, P, R","Q, P, S, R","Q, S, R, P"],answer:1,hint:"Data → chart → rules → follow-up.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A control chart is a time series plot with a centre line and ±3σ limits from the data (pp. 525–527); special-cause rules are then applied, and signals are investigated."},{title:"Apply",body:"Q (collect in order) → S (plot with limits) → P (apply tests) → R (investigate)."}],final:"Collect → plot → test → investigate = Q, S, P, R → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — nothing can be plotted before the data are collected.","","Incorrect — the tests are applied to a chart, so the chart must exist first.","Incorrect — investigation follows a signal, so the tests must come before it."],bookRef:"Book pp. 215, 525–533"},{id:"sv-2-1876",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`Order these data sets from the **narrowest** to the **broadest** inference space:

- **P** One month of production across all shifts
- **Q** One batch from one machine
- **R** One year across several material suppliers
- **S** One full shift covering several batches`,options:["S, Q, P, R","Q, S, P, R","Q, S, R, P","Q, P, S, R"],answer:1,hint:"The more time, shifts, batches and suppliers covered, the broader the inference space.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book (p. 212): short-term data have a narrow inference space (one shift, line, batch); long-term data are broad (many shifts, batches, suppliers, months)."},{title:"Apply",body:"Batch (Q) → shift (S) → month (P) → year with suppliers (R)."}],final:"Batch → shift → month → year = Q, S, P, R → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — one batch is narrower than a whole shift of several batches.","","Incorrect — a year across suppliers is broader than one month.","Incorrect — a single shift is narrower than a month of all shifts."],bookRef:"Book p. 212"},{id:"sv-2-1877",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"In the Measure phase, what does a baseline capability study “draw a line in the sand” for?",options:["The specification limits for the CTQ","The starting point against which improvement will be judged","The control limits for the Control plan","The sample size for the Gage R&R study"],answer:1,hint:"A line in the sand marks where you start.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book (p. 215): a capability study in Measure simply draws a line in the sand — it records the **baseline**."},{title:"Apply",body:"Later results are compared with that line to show improvement."}],final:"Line in the sand = baseline for comparison → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — specification limits come from the customer (VOC), not from a capability study.","","Incorrect — Control-phase limits are set after improvement, not by the Measure baseline.","Incorrect — Gage R&R sample size is a planning choice in MSA, unrelated to the baseline."],bookRef:"Book p. 215"},{id:"sv-2-1878",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Why does the book advise against trying to make a process stable before working on it?",options:["Stabilising first would invalidate the measurement system analysis","Unstable processes cannot be charted until the Control phase","It is a project because something is wrong with the process, so the team must first find out what","Capability can only be calculated on unstable data"],answer:2,hint:"Think about why a project exists in the first place.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book (p. 217): do not try to make your process stable before working on it — it is a project because something is wrong with it; go and find out what."},{title:"Apply",body:"Instability is part of the problem to study, not a precondition to fix first."}],final:"Instability is part of what the project studies → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — stabilising the process does not affect whether the gauge is valid.","Incorrect — unstable processes can and should be charted in Measure.","","Incorrect — capability is most meaningful on stable data, not only on unstable data."],bookRef:"Book p. 217"},{id:"sv-2-1879",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"What is another name for special-cause variation?",options:["Inherent variation","Assignable cause variation","Natural variation","Random variation"],answer:1,hint:"Special causes can be traced to a specific source.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book (pp. 158, 523): special cause = **assignable cause** = unnatural variability; common cause = natural variability, noise."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Inherent, natural and random all describe common cause."}],final:"Special = assignable → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — inherent variation is built into the system: common cause.","","Incorrect — natural variability is the book’s name for common cause.","Incorrect — random scatter around the average is common cause behaviour."],bookRef:"Book pp. 158, 523"},{id:"sv-2-1880",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which question does a control chart answer?",options:["Does the process meet the customer’s specification?","Is the process consistent and predictable over time?","Which input causes most of the defects?","Is the measurement system adequate for the process?"],answer:1,hint:"A control chart compares the process with itself, not with the customer.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A control chart checks **stability**: consistent, predictable output over time (pp. 211, 215)."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Capability compares with specifications; Analyze finds X’s; MSA checks the gauge."}],final:"Control chart = stability question → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — meeting the specification is answered by a capability analysis.","","Incorrect — finding the vital X is the job of Analyze tools.","Incorrect — measurement adequacy is answered by MSA (Gage R&R)."],bookRef:"Book pp. 211, 215, 518"},{id:"sv-2-1881",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"What did Deming’s funnel experiment illustrate about adjusting a stable process?",options:["Adjusting after each result in response to common cause variation increases the variation","Adjusting after each result keeps the process exactly on target","Leaving a process alone always causes it to drift away from target","Small adjustments are harmless as long as they stay inside the control limits"],answer:0,hint:"Marbles are dropped through a funnel; what happens when the funnel is moved after every drop?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Deming’s funnel: moving the funnel to compensate for where the last marble landed spreads the marbles more widely. This is **tampering** (p. 544)."},{title:"Apply",body:"For a stable process, the right action is to leave it alone or improve the system, not to react point by point."}],final:"Funnel = tampering increases variation → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the funnel shows the opposite: compensating for noise widens the spread.","Incorrect — leaving the funnel fixed over the target gave the smallest spread.","Incorrect — any adjustment made in response to common cause noise adds variation."],bookRef:"Book p. 544"},{id:"sv-2-1882",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"About how many data points does the book suggest a **long-term** data set should contain?",options:["At least 100–200","About 5–10","About 20–30","About 30–50"],answer:0,hint:"Long-term data must cover many shifts, batches and people.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book table (p. 212): short-term about 30–50 points (also 20–30); long-term **at least 100–200** points."},{title:"Apply",body:"The points must also be spread over a long period; volume alone does not make data long-term (p. 214)."}],final:"Long-term ≈ 100–200+ points → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — 5–10 points is far too few for any capability baseline.","Incorrect — 20–30 points is one of the book’s short-term guides.","Incorrect — 30–50 points is the book’s typical short-term guide."],bookRef:"Book pp. 212–214"},{id:"sv-2-1883",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"What proportion of points from a stable, normally distributed process is expected to fall inside ±3σ control limits?",options:["68.27%","95.45%","99.73%","99.99966%"],answer:2,hint:"Recall the empirical-rule figure for three standard deviations.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book (pp. 525–527): for a normal process, **99.73%** of points fall within ±3σ, so only about 0.27% fall outside by chance."},{title:"Apply",body:"That is why a point beyond the limits almost certainly has a special cause."}],final:"±3σ covers 99.73% → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — 68.27% lies within ±1σ.","Incorrect — 95.45% lies within ±2σ.","","Incorrect — this is the six sigma figure (3.4 DPMO), not the ±3σ coverage."],bookRef:"Book pp. 525–527"},{id:"sv-2-1884",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"What is tampering?",options:["Removing a special cause after it has been investigated","Adjusting a stable process in response to common cause variation","Recalculating control limits after a documented process change","Changing the specification limits to match the process"],answer:1,hint:"It is the error of treating noise as if it were a signal.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book (p. 544): reacting to common cause variation as if it were special — adjusting after every point — is **tampering**, and it increases variation."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"The other options are legitimate actions or unrelated to over-adjustment."}],final:"Tampering = adjusting for common cause → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — removing an investigated special cause is the correct response, not tampering.","","Incorrect — recalculating limits after a real, documented change is good practice.","Incorrect — moving specifications is a VOC issue, not the definition of tampering."],bookRef:"Book p. 544"},{id:"sv-2-1885",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Using the exam convention, which pattern on a control chart signals a shift in the process mean?",options:["6 points in a row steadily increasing","14 points in a row alternating up and down","1 point more than 3σ from the centre line","9 points in a row on the same side of the centre line"],answer:3,hint:"A shift keeps the points on one side of the centre line.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Exam convention (book pp. 532–533): **9 points in a row** on the same side of the centre line = shift (Test 2). Some rule sets use 8; the exam uses 9."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Trend = Test 3, alternating = Test 4, single point beyond 3σ = Test 1."}],final:"9 in a row = shift (Test 2) → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — 6 in a row increasing or decreasing is a trend (Test 3).","Incorrect — 14 alternating points indicate oscillation (Test 4).","Incorrect — one point beyond 3σ is an isolated out-of-control point (Test 1).",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 532–533"},{id:"sv-2-1886",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which control-chart pattern is MOST likely to be caused by gradual tool wear?",options:["Points alternating up and down","One isolated point beyond the upper control limit","Several points in a row steadily increasing","Points hugging the centre line"],answer:2,hint:"Wear builds up little by little. What pattern does that create?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Gradual wear moves the output a little more each time — a **trend** (6 or more points steadily increasing or decreasing, Test 3)."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Alternation, a single outlier and hugging have different typical causes."}],final:"Gradual wear = trend → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — alternation suggests over-adjustment or two alternating sources.","Incorrect — one isolated point suggests a one-off event, not gradual wear.","","Incorrect — hugging the centre line suggests stratification or inflated limits."],bookRef:"Book pp. 532–533"},{id:"sv-2-1887",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"How do special causes affect the predictability of a process?",options:["They make future output unpredictable because the process distribution changes over time","They make the process more predictable because the causes are known","They have no effect on predictability, only on the mean","They affect predictability only if the output is outside the specification"],answer:0,hint:"Stable means predictable. What do special causes do to stability?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A stable process has only common cause variation and is predictable (p. 211). Special causes shift, spread or distort the distribution, so the future cannot be predicted from the past."},{title:"Apply",body:"This is why capability from an unstable process is only a snapshot of the past."}],final:"Special causes → unpredictable → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — knowing a cause exists does not make output predictable while it keeps acting.","Incorrect — special causes change the mean, the spread or both, so predictions fail.","Incorrect — predictability is about consistency over time, regardless of specifications."],bookRef:"Book pp. 211, 517"},{id:"sv-2-1888",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"In the book’s Measure checklist, what does the question “Can the good signals be built into the process?” refer to?",options:["Common cause variation that should be reduced","Special causes that improve performance and should be kept","Points inside the control limits that should be ignored","Specification limits that should be tightened"],answer:1,hint:"A signal is a special cause. Which kind is worth keeping?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Checklist (p. 231): good signals (helpful special causes) should be built in; bad signals should be removed."},{title:"Apply",body:"Example: a new script that shifts handling time down is a good signal to standardise."}],final:"Good signal = helpful special cause to keep → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — common cause is not a “signal”; it is background noise.","","Incorrect — points inside the limits with no pattern are noise, not signals.","Incorrect — specification limits are VOC, not signals from the process."],bookRef:"Book p. 231"},{id:"sv-2-1889",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"As a rough guide in the book, how long a data period is usually treated as **short-term**?",options:["Two weeks or less","One month or more","One full quarter","At least one year"],answer:0,hint:"The book gives one rough cut-off for short-term and one for long-term.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book (p. 214): roughly, a month or more of data is long-term; **two weeks or less** is short-term."},{title:"Apply",body:"The time span (and the sources it covers) decides the class, not the number of units."}],final:"Two weeks or less = short-term → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — a month or more is the rough guide for long-term data.","Incorrect — a quarter covers many shifts and batches: long-term.","Incorrect — a year is clearly long-term data."],bookRef:"Book p. 214"},{id:"sv-2-1890",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"What is the PRIMARY purpose of assessing stability in the Measure phase?",options:["To set the specification limits for the CTQ","To remove special causes before the baseline is measured","To validate the measurement system","To know whether the baseline capability can be used to predict future performance"],answer:3,hint:"Capability numbers only predict the future under one condition.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Capability is most meaningful on a stable process (p. 211); an unstable process’s capability today does not predict tomorrow."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Specifications are VOC, stabilising first is discouraged (p. 217), and MSA comes earlier (p. 230)."}],final:"Stability tells you whether capability can predict → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — specifications come from the customer, not from a stability check.","Incorrect — the book says not to make the process stable before working on it.","Incorrect — the measurement system is validated by MSA before stability is assessed.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 211, 217, 230"},{id:"sv-2-1891",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about common cause and special cause variation is CORRECT?",options:["Special cause variation is part of the natural noise of the process","Common cause variation can only be reduced by changing the system itself","Common cause variation produces trends and sudden shifts","Special cause variation cannot be traced to any specific source"],answer:1,hint:"Common cause belongs to the system; special cause belongs to an event.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Common cause comes from many small unknown factors built into the system, so only a system change reduces it; special causes are assignable events (pp. 158, 523)."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"The other statements swap the properties of the two kinds of variation."}],final:"Reduce common cause by changing the system → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — natural noise is common cause; special cause is unnatural, assignable variation.","","Incorrect — trends and shifts are special-cause patterns.","Incorrect — special causes are assignable: they can be traced to a source."],bookRef:"Book pp. 158, 523"},{id:"sv-2-1892",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"Which statement about **process stability** and **measurement-system stability** is CORRECT?",options:["Both terms mean the same thing and are checked in the same study","Measurement stability is checked with a Gage R&R; process stability with a normality test","Process stability is consistent process output over time; measurement stability is consistent gauge bias over time","Process stability is bias across the measuring range; measurement stability is output over time"],answer:2,hint:"One kind of stability belongs to MSA, the other to the process.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book: measurement **stability** = bias over time (p. 183); **process** stability = consistent, predictable output over time (p. 211)."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Linearity is bias across the range; Gage R&R measures repeatability and reproducibility; normality is not a stability test."}],final:"Process stability ≠ gauge stability → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — one concerns the process output, the other the gauge; they are separate checks.","Incorrect — measurement stability is bias over time (tracked on control charts), and normality does not test stability.","","Incorrect — bias across the range is linearity, a measurement property."],trap:"“Stability” appears in both MSA (2.3.2) and process capability (2.4.2) with different meanings.",bookRef:"Book pp. 183, 211"},{id:"sv-2-1893",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about process entitlement is CORRECT?",options:["It is the long-term baseline performance of the process","It is the target value set by the customer","It is the best short-term performance the process has already demonstrated","It is the performance expected after the Improve phase"],answer:2,hint:"Look for the tightest stretch on a time series plot.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book (pp. 212, 215): short-term data show the optimal (**entitlement**) performance; the tightest region of a time series plot shows it."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Baseline = long-term; targets are VOC; post-Improve performance is not yet demonstrated."}],final:"Entitlement = best demonstrated short-term performance → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the baseline is long-term; entitlement is the best short-term performance.","Incorrect — a customer target is VOC, not something the process has demonstrated.","","Incorrect — entitlement has already been achieved; it is not a future projection."],bookRef:"Book pp. 212, 215"},{id:"sv-2-1894",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about a stable process over the long term is CORRECT?",options:["A stable process never drifts, so short-term and long-term performance are identical","Long-term performance of a stable process is better than its short-term performance","Drift over time only happens when special causes are present","Even a stable process drifts over time, so its long-term performance is worse than its short-term performance"],answer:3,hint:"Why does long-term performance look worse even without special causes?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book (pp. 213–214): long-term variation is always larger; even stable processes drift and shift by about 1.5 SD over time."},{title:"Apply",body:"So a short-term snapshot overstates what customers will see over months."}],final:"Stable processes still drift → long-term worse → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the book says even stable processes drift, by about 1.5 SD on average.","Incorrect — long-term variation is almost always larger, so long-term performance is worse.","Incorrect — drift occurs even in a stable process, which is why the 1.5 SD shift is used.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 213–214"},{id:"sv-2-1895",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which statement about a run chart is CORRECT?",options:["It plots data against the specification limits to estimate capability","It groups data into bars to show the shape of the distribution","It ranks causes from the most to the least frequent","It plots data in time order around the median to reveal non-random patterns"],answer:3,hint:"Run charts are a simple stability tool.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A run chart is a time series plot with a median line; Minitab tests it for clustering, mixtures, trends and oscillation — non-random patterns."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Capability report, histogram and Pareto chart are different tools."}],final:"Run chart = time order around the median → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — capability needs specification limits; a run chart has none.","Incorrect — bars showing shape describe a histogram.","Incorrect — ranking causes by frequency describes a Pareto chart.",""],bookRef:"Syllabus 2.4.2"},{id:"sv-2-1896",phase:2,topic:"2.4.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about the stability of attribute (pass/fail) data is CORRECT?",options:["Stability can be assessed for attribute data by charting them over time, for example on a p chart","Stability applies only to continuous data","Attribute data are stable by definition because each unit only passes or fails","Attribute data must be converted to a Z-score before they can be charted"],answer:0,hint:"Stability means consistency over time — for any kind of data.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Stability is established by charting the output over time (p. 215). Attribute outputs are charted with attribute charts such as the p chart."},{title:"Apply",body:"A defect rate can drift or spike; it must be stable before an overall rate is trusted as capability."}],final:"Attribute data can be charted for stability → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — any process output, continuous or attribute, can be checked for consistency over time.","Incorrect — pass/fail rates can shift, trend or spike like any other output.","Incorrect — attribute data are charted directly as proportions or counts."],bookRef:"Book p. 215"},{id:"sv-2-1897",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:2,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

Attribute data are always treated as long-term data, in the [?] condition.`,options:["baseline","centred","shifted","short-term"],answer:2,hint:"Long-term data already include the drift of about 1.5 standard deviations.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book (p. 223): “Attribute data is always long-term in the **shifted** condition”, because many samples over a long time are needed to estimate a defect rate."},{title:"Apply",body:"That is why the Z from attribute data is a long-term Z, and 1.5 is added to report short-term capability."}],final:"Attribute data = long-term, shifted → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a baseline is long-term, but the book’s phrase describes the 1.5σ condition, not the baseline.","Incorrect — centring refers to the mean relative to the specification, not to the time condition of the data.","","Incorrect — the opposite: attribute data are long-term, and 1.5 is added to estimate short-term."],bookRef:"Book p. 223"},{id:"sv-2-1898",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

A [?] is a whole unit that fails to meet requirements; it may contain one or more defects.`,options:["defect","defective","nonconformity","opportunity"],answer:1,hint:"Separate the failed unit from the individual flaws on it.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book (p. 530): a **defect** is a failure to meet any one requirement; a **defective** is a whole unit that fails, and it may contain one or more defects."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Defect and nonconformity describe single flaws; an opportunity is a chance for a flaw."}],final:"Whole failed unit = defective → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a defect is one failure to meet a single requirement, not the whole failed unit.","","Incorrect — a nonconformity is another word for a single defect, not the unit.","Incorrect — an opportunity is a chance for a defect to occur, not a failed unit."],bookRef:"Book p. 530"},{id:"sv-2-1899",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

Attribute capability starts by turning the count of defects or defectives into a [?], such as DPU or the proportion defective.`,options:["chart","histogram","rate","score"],answer:2,hint:"DPU and proportion defective are both “per unit” figures.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book steps (p. 222): after collecting data, **calculate DPU** (a rate), then find the Z-score, then convert to Cp/Cpk."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Charts check stability, histograms describe continuous shape, and the Z-score follows the rate."}],final:"Count → rate → Z → Cpk → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a chart (p or u) checks stability; capability itself starts from a rate.","Incorrect — a histogram shows the shape of continuous data, not a defect rate.","","Incorrect — a score (Z) comes later, from the rate, using the Z table."],bookRef:"Book p. 222"},{id:"sv-2-1900",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"statements",difficulty:2,stem:`Which statements about attribute capability are CORRECT?

1. Capability can be reported for pass/fail data by using a Z-score found from the defect rate.
2. A normality test is the key step before converting attribute data to Cpk.
3. The 1.5σ shift is subtracted from attribute Z to report short-term capability.
4. The Z-score is found by looking up the defect rate in the Z table.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:3,hint:"Attribute capability goes from a defect rate to Z, not through a normality test.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book steps (p. 222): calculate DPU → find the Z-score from the Z table → convert Z to Cp/Cpk. The data are long-term, so Z(ST) = Z(LT) + 1.5 (p. 223)."},{title:"Apply",body:"Statements 1 and 4 are true; 2 (normality) and 3 (subtract) are false."}],final:"Statements 1 and 4 are correct → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — statement 2 is false: attribute data are not normal, so the normality step is replaced.","Incorrect — both are false: there is no normality step, and 1.5 is added, not subtracted.","Incorrect — statement 3 is false: attribute Z is long-term, so 1.5 is added for short-term.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 221–223"},{id:"sv-2-1901",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"statements",difficulty:2,stem:`Which statements about Z-scores in attribute capability are CORRECT?

1. A larger Z-score means a smaller proportion of defects.
2. The Z-score can be read from the Z table by finding the defect proportion.
3. The long-term Z is larger than the short-term Z.
4. The Cpk is equal to the Z-score itself.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:0,hint:"Z is a distance in standard deviations; more distance means less tail area.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The further the limit is from the mean (larger Z), the smaller the tail area. Attribute Z is found by a reverse table look-up (p. 222)."},{title:"Apply",body:"Statements 1 and 2 are true. Long-term Z is smaller than short-term (p. 223), and Cpk = Z(ST) ÷ 3 (p. 224)."}],final:"Statements 1 and 2 are correct → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — statement 3 is false: Z(ST) = Z(LT) + 1.5, so the long-term Z is smaller.","Incorrect — both are false: long-term Z is smaller, and Cpk is Z(ST) divided by 3.","Incorrect — statement 4 is false: Cpk is Z(ST) divided by 3, not Z itself."],bookRef:"Book pp. 222–224"},{id:"sv-2-1902",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"Which is NOT one of the book’s attribute capability steps?",options:["Calculate DPU","Find the Z-score from the Z table","Convert the Z-score to Cp and Cpk","Test the data for normality"],answer:3,hint:"Where does attribute data “hit a wall” in the continuous roadmap?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book (pp. 221–222): continuous capability includes a normality check, but attribute data hit a wall there; the attribute steps use **DPU → Z → Cp/Cpk** instead."},{title:"Apply",body:"The normality test is the step that is replaced, so it is the odd one out."}],final:"Normality test is not an attribute step → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — calculating DPU is step 5 of the attribute steps.","Incorrect — finding Z from the Z table is step 6 of the attribute steps.","Incorrect — converting Z to Cp and Cpk is the final attribute step.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 221–222"},{id:"sv-2-1903",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:1,stem:"Which is NOT an attribute measure of process performance?",options:["DPMO","DPU","Proportion defective","StDev (Within)"],answer:3,hint:"Which option needs measured, continuous values?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Attribute capability uses counts: DPU, DPMO and proportion defective (pp. 221–225)."},{title:"Apply",body:"StDev (Within) is the short-term standard deviation of continuous data used for Cp and Cpk — not an attribute measure."}],final:"StDev (Within) belongs to continuous capability → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — DPMO counts defects per million opportunities: an attribute measure.","Incorrect — DPU counts defects per unit: an attribute measure.","Incorrect — proportion defective counts failed units: an attribute measure.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 221–225"},{id:"sv-2-1904",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement is NOT true of defect (Poisson) data?",options:["One unit can contain several defects","Results are summarised as defects per unit (DPU)","Defects are counted within a defined unit or area of opportunity","Each unit is classified only as good or bad"],answer:3,hint:"Good/bad classification belongs to the other attribute model.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Poisson: counts of defects per unit, summarised as DPU. Binomial: each unit good or bad, summarised as proportion defective."},{title:"Apply",body:"Classifying units only as good or bad describes binomial (defectives) data."}],final:"Good/bad units = binomial, not Poisson → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — this is true: Poisson data allow several defects on one unit.","Incorrect — this is true: DPU is the summary of a Poisson capability study.","Incorrect — this is true: defects are counted in a defined unit or area.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 221–225, 530"},{id:"sv-2-1905",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"Which data set would NOT suit a binomial (proportion defective) capability analysis?",options:["Calls answered or abandoned","Orders delivered on time or late","The number of scratches counted on each painted panel","SIM activations that succeeded or failed"],answer:2,hint:"Binomial needs each unit classified as good or bad.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Pass/fail classification of units → binomial (proportion defective). Counts of flaws per unit → Poisson (DPU)."},{title:"Apply",body:"A panel can carry many scratches, so scratches per panel are defect counts — a Poisson (DPU) analysis."}],final:"Scratches per panel = defect counts (Poisson) → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — each call is either answered or abandoned: pass/fail, so binomial.","Incorrect — each order is either on time or late: pass/fail, so binomial.","","Incorrect — each activation succeeds or fails: pass/fail, so binomial."],bookRef:"Book pp. 221–225"},{id:"sv-2-1906",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A team plans a binomial capability study of solder joints judged pass/fail by **four visual inspectors**. Which study should be completed first?",options:["An attribute agreement analysis of the inspectors’ pass/fail decisions","A crossed Gage R&R on measured joint dimensions","A normality test on the number of failed joints","A regression of failures on inspection time"],answer:0,hint:"The measurement system must be trusted before any capability is calculated.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Measure roadmap: accept the measurement system before stability and capability (p. 230). For pass/fail judgements, that is an **attribute agreement analysis**."},{title:"Apply",body:"If inspectors disagree on what fails, the proportion defective is meaningless."}],final:"Attribute MSA before attribute capability → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — Gage R&R is for continuous measurements; these decisions are pass/fail.","Incorrect — attribute counts are not tested for normality; that step is replaced in attribute capability.","Incorrect — regression explores X’s in Analyze; it does not validate the inspectors."],bookRef:"Book pp. 199–203, 230"},{id:"sv-2-1907",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"Two billing teams are compared. Team A’s invoices have **4** checked fields; Team B’s have **20**. Management wants a fair comparison of their quality. Which measure should be used?",options:["DPMO","DPU","Number of defects","Number of defective invoices"],answer:0,hint:"Which measure adjusts for the number of chances to fail on each unit?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"When units have different numbers of opportunities, **DPMO** makes processes comparable because it counts opportunities."},{title:"Apply",body:"A 20-field invoice has five times as many chances to fail as a 4-field invoice; DPMO allows for that."}],final:"Different complexity → DPMO → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — DPU ignores how many fields can fail, so complex invoices look worse.","Incorrect — a raw count ignores both volume and complexity.","Incorrect — counting defective invoices ignores the different number of chances to fail."],bookRef:"Book pp. 221–225"},{id:"sv-2-1908",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"Pins are checked with a **go/no-go gauge**, so each pin simply passes or fails. An engineer wants to report Cp and Cpk using the normal capability formulas. What is the problem?",options:["Go/no-go results are attribute data, so capability should come from the proportion defective via Z","Go/no-go gauges cannot be used in the Measure phase","Cp and Cpk can only be calculated for service processes","The pins must first be shown to be normally distributed by counting failures"],answer:0,hint:"What type of data does a go/no-go gauge produce?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Continuous formulas need a mean and standard deviation; with attribute data we hit a wall at the normality step (p. 221)."},{title:"Apply",body:"Use the attribute route: proportion defective → Z → Cp/Cpk (p. 222)."}],final:"Go/no-go = attribute → proportion → Z → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — go/no-go gauges are common Measure-phase tools; the issue is the data type.","Incorrect — Cp and Cpk are used in manufacturing and service alike, for continuous data.","Incorrect — failure counts are not normally distributed; the normality step is replaced."],bookRef:"Book pp. 221–222"},{id:"sv-2-1909",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"An inspector checks a customer application form and finds **three** errors on it. How is this form counted?",options:["As one defective unit containing three defects","As three defective units","As three defectives and one defect","As one defect containing three defectives"],answer:0,hint:"Count units for defectives and flaws for defects.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book (p. 530): a defect is a failure to meet one requirement; a defective is a whole unit that fails and may contain several defects."},{title:"Apply",body:"One form with three errors = **one defective, three defects**."}],final:"One form, three errors = 1 defective, 3 defects → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the form is one unit, so it can be only one defective.","Incorrect — this reverses the terms: each error is a defect, the form is the defective.","Incorrect — this reverses the terms: a defective contains defects, not the other way round."],bookRef:"Book p. 530"},{id:"sv-2-1910",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:"A quarter of SIM activation data gives 4% defective. The p chart shows a huge spike in week 6, during a network migration. The Green Belt reports **4% as the process capability** without comment. Was this an appropriate action?",options:["Yes, because an overall proportion always includes every week","Yes, because attribute data are long-term and include special causes by definition","No, because the rate is not stable, so the special cause in week 6 should be acknowledged and investigated","No, because a p chart cannot be used with attribute capability data"],answer:2,hint:"An overall rate is only a fair capability if the rate is stable over time.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Capability is most meaningful on a stable process (p. 211), and special causes must be acknowledged (p. 231)."},{title:"Apply",body:"The migration spike mixes two different conditions into one figure; report it with the caveat and investigate week 6."}],final:"Unstable rate → acknowledge the special cause → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — including every week hides the fact that week 6 behaved differently.","Incorrect — long-term data include special causes, but these must still be acknowledged, not hidden.","","Incorrect — the p chart is exactly the tool for checking a proportion’s stability."],bookRef:"Book pp. 211, 231"},{id:"sv-2-1911",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:2,stem:"Halfway through an attribute capability study, the team finds that two inspectors have used different definitions of an “error” on order forms. What is the BEST action?",options:["Agree one operational definition of an error and re-inspect before calculating capability","Average the two inspectors’ defect counts and continue","Keep the higher defect count to be safe and continue","Switch to DPMO so that the definitions no longer matter"],answer:0,hint:"Which attribute capability step defines what counts as a defect?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book steps (p. 222): verify customer requirements and **validate the specification** — what counts as a defect — before collecting data."},{title:"Apply",body:"Inconsistent definitions make the defect rate meaningless, so fix the definition and re-collect."}],final:"Fix the defect definition first → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — averaging two different definitions gives a number that matches neither.","Incorrect — picking one count does not fix the inconsistent definition behind the data.","Incorrect — DPMO depends even more on clear definitions of defects and opportunities."],bookRef:"Book p. 222"},{id:"sv-2-1912",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:"A team finds **1.6 errors per form** on loan application forms, each with 15 checkable fields. It plans to look up DPU = 1.6 directly in the Z table. What is the BEST action?",options:["Convert to DPMO (defects per opportunity) or to the proportion of defective forms before the Z look-up","Look up 1.6 directly, as the book does for its call-centre example","Subtract 1.5 from the DPU before the look-up","Report DPU = 1.6 as the short-term Cpk"],answer:0,hint:"A Z-table look-up needs a proportion between 0 and 1.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"DPU can be looked up directly only when each unit has one opportunity, so DPU is a proportion (p. 224). With 15 fields per form, DPU can exceed 1."},{title:"Apply",body:"Convert to a per-opportunity rate (DPMO) or to the proportion of forms with at least one error first."}],final:"DPU > 1 → convert before the Z look-up → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the book’s call has one opportunity, so its DPU is a proportion; 1.6 cannot be a tail area.","Incorrect — the 1.5 shift adjusts Z values, not DPU.","Incorrect — DPU is a defect rate, not a capability index."],bookRef:"Book pp. 222–224"},{id:"sv-2-1913",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A stable p chart supports this capability output for failed SIM activations:

\`\`\`
Binomial Process Capability Analysis: Failed Activations

Summary Stats (using 95.0% confidence)
Total samples         20000
Total defectives        340
%Defective:            1.70     Lower CI:   1.53     Upper CI:   1.89
PPM Def:              17000     Lower CI:  15254     Upper CI:  18888
Process Z:           2.1201     Lower CI: 2.0773     Upper CI: 2.1634
\`\`\`

Which statement is supported by this output?`,options:["Exactly 1.70% of all future activations will fail","Between 1.53 and 1.89 activations fail out of every million","The process Cpk lies between 2.08 and 2.16","With 95% confidence, between about 1.53% and 1.89% of activations fail"],answer:3,hint:"Match each confidence interval to the statistic and units on its row.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"%Defective = 1.70 with Lower CI 1.53 and Upper CI 1.89 (95% confidence)."},{title:"Interpret",body:"So the true failure rate is estimated to lie between about 1.53% and 1.89%. PPM and Z rows have their own intervals."}],final:"CI for %Defective = 1.53% to 1.89% → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — 1.70% is a sample estimate; the confidence interval shows its uncertainty.","Incorrect — the CI is in percent; per million the bounds are the PPM figures (15,254 to 18,888).","Incorrect — 2.08 to 2.16 is the CI for Process Z, not for Cpk.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 221–225"},{id:"sv-2-1914",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`\`\`\`
Poisson Capability Analysis: Errors per Customer Contract

Summary Stats (using 95.0% confidence)
Total units inspected     2500
Total defects              120
Mean DPU:               0.0480     Lower CI:  0.0398     Upper CI:  0.0574
Min DPU:                0.0000
Max DPU:                0.1200
Target DPU:             0.0000
\`\`\`

What kind of data does this analysis assume?`,options:["Counts of defects on each unit, where one unit can carry several defects","Pass/fail classification of each unit as good or defective","Continuous measurements with a mean and standard deviation","Ranked scores from customer satisfaction surveys"],answer:0,hint:"Poisson capability reports DPU. What does DPU count?",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"The report gives Mean DPU 0.0480 from 120 defects on 2,500 units."},{title:"Interpret",body:"Poisson capability is for **defects per unit**: counts of flaws where one unit may have several. Pass/fail data go to binomial capability."}],final:"Poisson capability = defect counts per unit → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — pass/fail units are analysed with binomial capability (%Defective).","Incorrect — continuous data use a normal capability analysis with Cp and Cpk.","Incorrect — ranked scores are ordinal data, not defect counts."],bookRef:"Book pp. 221–225"},{id:"sv-2-1915",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`The book’s sigma table is shown below:

\`\`\`
Sigma level (Z ST)   Short-term DPMO   Long-term DPMO
        3                1,350             66,807
        4                   31.7            6,210
        5                    0.3              233
        6                    0.0              3.4
\`\`\`

A billing process is reported as a **4 sigma** process. Which value describes the defect rate customers should expect over time?`,options:["31.7 DPMO","233 DPMO","6,210 DPMO","66,807 DPMO"],answer:2,hint:"Customers experience the process over time, with the 1.5σ drift included.",steps:[{title:"Read the table",body:"The 4 sigma row gives 31.7 DPMO short-term and **6,210 DPMO long-term**."},{title:"Interpret",body:"Over time the process drifts about 1.5σ, so customers see the long-term (shifted) column — the same column that gives six sigma = 3.4 DPMO."}],final:"4σ long-term = 6,210 DPMO → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — 31.7 is the short-term (unshifted) DPMO, which ignores long-term drift.","Incorrect — 233 DPMO is the long-term rate of a 5 sigma process.","","Incorrect — 66,807 DPMO is the long-term rate of a 3 sigma process."],bookRef:"Book p. 223"},{id:"sv-2-1916",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:1,stem:`An audit of onboarding forms gives this summary:

\`\`\`
Audit Summary - Customer Onboarding Forms
Forms inspected                 600
Forms with at least one error    42
Total errors found               57
Fields checked per form          12
\`\`\`

Which statement correctly describes these data?`,options:["The 57 errors are defects, and the 42 forms are defectives","The 42 forms are defects, and the 57 errors are defectives","Each of the 600 forms is one opportunity","The 12 fields are the defective units"],answer:0,hint:"Units, flaws and chances: which is which?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Defect = one failure against a requirement; defective = a whole unit with at least one defect (p. 530); opportunities = chances per unit for a defect."},{title:"Read the output",body:"57 errors = defects; 42 forms with errors = defectives; 12 fields = opportunities per form."}],final:"57 defects, 42 defectives, 12 opportunities → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — this reverses the terms: errors are defects, and failed forms are defectives.","Incorrect — each form has 12 opportunities (fields), not one.","Incorrect — the fields are opportunities; the forms are the units."],bookRef:"Book p. 530"},{id:"sv-2-1917",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A team checks whether its process meets a target of **0.50% defective**:

\`\`\`
Binomial Process Capability Analysis: Incorrect Shipments

Summary Stats (using 95.0% confidence)
Total samples          8000
Total defectives         96
%Defective:            1.20     Lower CI:   0.97     Upper CI:   1.46
Target:                0.50
PPM Def:              12000     Lower CI:   9731     Upper CI:  14635
Process Z:           2.2571     Lower CI: 2.1798     Upper CI: 2.3366
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is correct?`,options:["The process meets the target, because the lower CI is close to 0.50","The process meets the target, because Process Z is above 2","The %defective is above the target, even at the lower confidence bound","No conclusion is possible, because binomial capability has no target"],answer:2,hint:"Compare the whole confidence interval for %defective with the target.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"%Defective = 1.20 with a 95% CI of 0.97 to 1.46; target = 0.50."},{title:"Interpret",body:"Even the lower bound (0.97%) is above 0.50%, so the process does not meet the target."}],final:"Whole CI above target → target not met → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the lower bound is 0.97%, almost double the 0.50% target.","Incorrect — a Process Z above 2 does not show that the 0.50% target is met.","","Incorrect — the output prints a target of 0.50, which can be compared with the CI."],bookRef:"Book pp. 221–225"},{id:"sv-2-1918",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"Capability studies can be carried out on both discrete and continuous data.",options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:"Recall whether attribute data need their own capability method or none at all.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book (p. 211): capability studies can be done on **both discrete and continuous** data."},{title:"Apply",body:"Discrete data use the attribute route (DPU → Z → Cp/Cpk) instead of the normal-curve formulas."}],final:"Both data types can be studied → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the book states that capability studies can be done on both discrete and continuous data."],bookRef:"Book pp. 211, 222"},{id:"sv-2-1919",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`Put the first steps of the book’s attribute capability method in order:

- **P** Collect sample data
- **Q** Select the output for improvement
- **R** Validate the specification limits (what counts as a defect)
- **S** Verify the customer requirements`,options:["Q, S, R, P","S, Q, R, P","Q, R, S, P","Q, S, P, R"],answer:0,hint:"Decide what to study, what the customer wants, what a defect is — then collect.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book steps (p. 222): 1 select the output → 2 verify customer requirements → 3 validate the specification limits → 4 collect sample data → then DPU → Z → Cp/Cpk."},{title:"Apply",body:"Q, S, R, P."}],final:"Select → verify → validate → collect = Q, S, R, P → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the output must be chosen before its requirements can be verified.","Incorrect — the requirements are verified before the specification is validated against them.","Incorrect — what counts as a defect must be settled before data are collected."],bookRef:"Book p. 222"},{id:"sv-2-1920",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`A team prepares to track DPMO for a quoting process. Put these preparation steps in order:

- **P** Count the number of opportunities on each quote
- **Q** Define the unit (one customer quote)
- **R** Inspect quotes and count the defects found
- **S** Define what counts as a defect on a quote`,options:["Q, S, P, R","S, Q, P, R","Q, P, R, S","Q, S, R, P"],answer:0,hint:"Unit → defect → opportunities → data.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Operational definitions come first: the unit, then what is a defect, then how many opportunities each unit has — all fixed before inspection."},{title:"Apply",body:"Q (unit) → S (defect) → P (opportunities) → R (inspect and count)."}],final:"Unit → defect → opportunities → count = Q, S, P, R → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — a defect cannot be defined until the unit it applies to is defined.","Incorrect — counting defects before defining a defect gives inconsistent data.","Incorrect — opportunities must be fixed before inspection so DPMO is consistent."],bookRef:"Book pp. 221–225, 530"},{id:"sv-2-1921",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"Which of these processes is performing BEST?",options:["A process with an attribute Cpk of 0.87","A process at 4.5 sigma (short-term)","A process with a long-term Z of 1.11","A process at 66,807 DPMO long-term"],answer:1,hint:"Put every option on the sigma scale using facts from the book.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book facts: 66,807 DPMO long-term = 3σ (p. 223); Z(LT) 1.11 and Cpk 0.87 are the call-centre results, about 2.6σ (p. 224)."},{title:"Apply",body:"Only the 4.5 sigma process is above 3σ, so it performs best."}],final:"4.5σ beats 3σ and 2.6σ → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — Cpk 0.87 is the book’s call-centre result, about 2.6 sigma.","","Incorrect — Z(LT) 1.11 is also the call centre, about 2.6 sigma short-term.","Incorrect — 66,807 DPMO long-term is a 3 sigma process."],bookRef:"Book pp. 223–224"},{id:"sv-2-1922",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which CTQ would need an **attribute** capability analysis rather than a continuous one?",options:["Time taken to complete a number-port request, in minutes","Signal strength at the customer premises, in dBm","Whether each number-port request completes successfully","Call set-up time, in milliseconds"],answer:2,hint:"Attribute data are counts or pass/fail results, not measurements.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Capability can be done on discrete and continuous data (p. 211); pass/fail outcomes need the attribute route (p. 221)."},{title:"Apply",body:"Success or failure of each request is pass/fail data; the others are measured on a continuous scale."}],final:"Pass/fail outcome → attribute capability → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — minutes are a continuous measurement, suited to Cp and Cpk.","Incorrect — dBm readings are continuous measurements.","","Incorrect — milliseconds are a continuous measurement."],bookRef:"Book pp. 211, 221"},{id:"sv-2-1923",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"What does DPU measure?",options:["The proportion of units that have at least one defect","The number of defects per million opportunities","The proportion of units that pass inspection","The average number of defects found on each unit"],answer:3,hint:"Defects per unit: defects on top, units below.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"DPU = total defects ÷ total units: the average number of defects per unit."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Proportion defective counts failed units; DPMO uses opportunities; yield counts good units."}],final:"DPU = defects per unit → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — this is the proportion defective, which counts units rather than defects.","Incorrect — this is DPMO, which divides by opportunities.","Incorrect — this is yield.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 222–224"},{id:"sv-2-1924",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"In the book’s call-centre example, which index does it say should be used to judge the ability to meet CTQs?",options:["Ppk, the long-term index","Pp, the long-term potential index","Cpk, the short-term index","DPU, the defect rate"],answer:2,hint:"The book gives each index a different job.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book (pp. 224–225): use **Cpk** to judge the ability to meet CTQs; Ppk shows how much variation the process really shows over time."},{title:"Apply",body:"The call centre’s Cpk of 0.87 is compared with the desired 1.33."}],final:"Judge CTQs with Cpk → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the book says Ppk shows how much variation the process really shows over time.","Incorrect — Pp is a continuous potential index; the example reports Cpk and Ppk.","","Incorrect — DPU is the starting rate, not a capability index."],bookRef:"Book pp. 224–225"},{id:"sv-2-1925",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"In the book’s call-centre example, why is Ppk lower than Cpk?",options:["Ppk uses the within-subgroup standard deviation","Ppk ignores the customer requirement","Ppk uses the long-term Z, which is 1.5 lower than the short-term Z","Ppk is calculated only from the dropped calls"],answer:2,hint:"Which Z feeds each index?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book (p. 224): Ppk = Z(LT) ÷ 3 and Cpk = Z(ST) ÷ 3, where Z(ST) = Z(LT) + 1.5."},{title:"Apply",body:"The long-term Z is smaller, so Ppk (0.37) is lower than Cpk (0.87)."}],final:"Ppk uses the smaller long-term Z → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — within-subgroup SD belongs to continuous Cp/Cpk; attribute data have no subgroups here.","Incorrect — both indices compare the process with the same requirement.","","Incorrect — both indices come from the same dropped-call proportion."],bookRef:"Book pp. 224–225"},{id:"sv-2-1926",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which distribution describes the number of defective units in a fixed number of pass/fail units?",options:["Binomial","Exponential","Normal","Poisson"],answer:0,hint:"Each unit is good or bad — two outcomes.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Pass/fail units with a fixed sample size and the same chance of failing → **binomial** (proportion defective)."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Poisson = defects per unit; normal = continuous data; exponential = time between events."}],final:"Pass/fail units = binomial → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the exponential models times between events, not counts of defective units.","Incorrect — the normal describes continuous measurements.","Incorrect — the Poisson describes counts of defects per unit, where one unit can have several."],bookRef:"Book pp. 221–225"},{id:"sv-2-1927",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"If a sigma level is quoted without saying whether it is short-term or long-term, which convention is usually assumed?",options:["The short-term sigma level, as in the first column of the sigma table","The long-term Z with no shift applied","The Ppk multiplied by three","The proportion defective expressed in standard deviations"],answer:0,hint:"Which column of the sigma table is labelled “sigma level”?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s sigma table lists the **sigma level as Z(ST)**, with the short-term and long-term DPMO beside it (p. 223)."},{title:"Apply",body:"So an unlabelled sigma level is taken as short-term (the shifted convention)."}],final:"Unlabelled sigma level = short-term → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — a long-term Z is normally labelled as such; quoted sigma levels are short-term.","Incorrect — Ppk × 3 is the long-term Z, not the usual quoted sigma level.","Incorrect — a proportion is not a number of standard deviations."],bookRef:"Book p. 223"},{id:"sv-2-1928",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"In attribute capability, what is the purpose of validating the specification limits?",options:["To agree exactly what counts as a defect before data are collected","To calculate the control limits for the p chart","To check that the defect counts are normally distributed","To decide whether to add or subtract the 1.5σ shift"],answer:0,hint:"For pass/fail data, the specification is the definition of failure.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book steps (p. 222): validate the specification limits — for attribute data this defines **what counts as a defect**."},{title:"Apply",body:"Without an agreed definition, inspectors count different things and DPU is meaningless."}],final:"Validate spec = define a defect → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — control limits are calculated from the data, not from specifications.","Incorrect — attribute data are not normal, and normality is not an attribute step.","Incorrect — the shift direction depends on long- versus short-term, not on the specification."],bookRef:"Book p. 222"},{id:"sv-2-1929",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"What is a defect?",options:["A failure to meet any one requirement of a unit","A whole unit that fails inspection","A chance for an error to occur on a unit","A unit that passes all requirements"],answer:0,hint:"Single requirement or whole unit?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book (p. 530): a **defect** is a failure to meet any one requirement; a defective is the whole unit that fails."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Defective, opportunity and good unit are the neighbouring terms."}],final:"Defect = one failed requirement → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — a whole failed unit is a defective.","Incorrect — a chance for an error is an opportunity.","Incorrect — a unit that passes everything is a good unit, counted in yield."],bookRef:"Book p. 530"},{id:"sv-2-1930",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Roughly what sigma level corresponds to the usual minimum Cpk of 1.33?",options:["About 1.33 sigma","About 3 sigma","About 4 sigma","About 6 sigma"],answer:2,hint:"Recall the book’s link between the desired Cpk and the sigma level.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book (p. 225): the desired Cpk of **1.33** is about **4 sigma**."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Cpk 1.0 ≈ 3 sigma and Cpk 2.0 ≈ 6 sigma."}],final:"Cpk 1.33 ≈ 4σ → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — Cpk is about Z(ST) divided by 3, so 1.33 is not itself the sigma level.","Incorrect — 3 sigma corresponds to Cpk 1.0.","","Incorrect — 6 sigma corresponds to Cpk 2.0."],bookRef:"Book p. 225"},{id:"sv-2-1931",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Why does the book convert attribute results into Cpk and Ppk?",options:["To express pass/fail performance on the same scale as continuous capability","To prove that attribute data are normally distributed","To remove the need for a stable process","To turn defects into defectives"],answer:0,hint:"A common scale lets different CTQs be compared.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The attribute route ends by converting Z to Cp/Cpk and Pp/Ppk (pp. 222–224), giving the same language as continuous capability."},{title:"Apply",body:"A failed-activation rate can then be compared with a continuous CTQ’s Cpk."}],final:"Common capability scale → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the conversion does not make the data normal; it uses the Z table instead.","Incorrect — an overall defect rate is still only fair if the process is stable.","Incorrect — defects and defectives are counted before the conversion; Cpk does not change them."],bookRef:"Book pp. 222–224"},{id:"sv-2-1932",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"In the book’s example (mean 50, SD 3, USL 54), the Z table gives **0.09176** for Z = 1.33. What does this value represent?",options:["The proportion of output expected inside the specification","The Cpk of the process","The number of standard deviations between the mean and the USL","The proportion of output expected beyond the upper specification limit"],answer:3,hint:"In the forward direction the Z table turns a Z-score into a tail area.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book (p. 222): Z = 1.33 gives a tail area of 0.09176 — about 9.2% beyond the USL, or 91,760 PPM."},{title:"Apply",body:"Attribute capability uses the same table in reverse: proportion in, Z out."}],final:"Table value = tail area beyond the limit → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — inside the limit would be the larger remaining area, not 0.09176.","Incorrect — Cpk is an index from Z divided by 3, not a table area.","Incorrect — that distance is the Z-score itself (1.33), not the table value.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 222"},{id:"sv-2-1933",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Why does attribute capability usually need far more units than a continuous capability study of the same process?",options:["Attribute data must always be collected over at least one year","Minitab requires at least 10,000 units for any attribute analysis","Each pass/fail result carries less information than a measurement, so many more results are needed to estimate the rate","Attribute data contain more measurement error than continuous data"],answer:2,hint:"A measurement says how far a unit is from the limit; a pass/fail result does not.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Continuous data are always more desirable (p. 146). Attribute data need many samples over a long time to estimate a defect rate with confidence (p. 223)."},{title:"Apply",body:"A “pass” near the limit and a “pass” at the target look identical, so more units are needed."}],final:"Less information per unit → more units → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the book asks for many samples over a long time, but sets no one-year rule.","Incorrect — there is no fixed software minimum; the need comes from the information per unit.","","Incorrect — attribute inspection can be reliable; the issue is information, not error."],bookRef:"Book pp. 146, 223"},{id:"sv-2-1934",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"In the book’s call-centre example, what counted as a **defective**?",options:["A call that lasted longer than average","A call dropped before it was answered","Each error made by an agent during a call","A call answered by a new agent"],answer:1,hint:"Recall the 2,666 out of 20,000.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book (p. 224): 20,000 calls in a month; **2,666 dropped before being answered** — the defectives."},{title:"Apply",body:"Each call either drops or not, so DPU equals the proportion defective here."}],final:"Defective = call dropped before answer → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a long call is not the defect defined in the example.","","Incorrect — agent errors would be defects per call; the example counts dropped calls.","Incorrect — who answered the call is not the defect definition used."],bookRef:"Book p. 224"},{id:"sv-2-1935",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which control chart checks that the defects per unit are stable before a Poisson capability result is trusted?",options:["I-MR chart","P chart","U chart","Xbar-R chart"],answer:2,hint:"Match the chart to the statistic: proportion defective or defects per unit.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Poisson capability reports DPU, so its stability is checked on a **U chart** (defects per unit over time); binomial capability uses a P chart."},{title:"Apply",body:"An overall DPU is only a fair capability estimate if the U chart is stable (p. 211)."}],final:"Defects per unit → U chart → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the I-MR chart is for individual continuous measurements.","Incorrect — the P chart tracks the proportion defective (binomial), not defects per unit.","","Incorrect — the Xbar-R chart is for subgrouped continuous measurements."],bookRef:"Book p. 211; Syllabus 2.4.3"},{id:"sv-2-1936",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which statement about attribute capability in the Measure phase is CORRECT?",options:["It replaces the need for a measurement system analysis","It gives a baseline sigma level against which later improvement can be judged","It can only be carried out after the Improve phase","It needs subgrouped continuous data to be calculated"],answer:1,hint:"What is any capability study in Measure used for?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Capability analysis gives a **baseline metric** for improvement (p. 207); attribute data reach it through DPU → Z → Cpk (p. 222)."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"It does not replace MSA, it is not post-Improve, and it needs no continuous data."}],final:"Attribute capability = Measure baseline → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the inspection system must be checked (attribute agreement) before capability.","","Incorrect — the baseline is set in Measure, before improvement.","Incorrect — attribute capability uses counts, not continuous subgroups."],bookRef:"Book pp. 207, 222"},{id:"sv-2-1937",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about the book’s call-centre example is CORRECT?",options:["It is about a 6 sigma process, with a Cpk above 2","It is about a 4 sigma process, with a Cpk of 1.33","It shows that Ppk is higher than Cpk for attribute data","It is about a 2.6 sigma process, with a Cpk well below 1.33"],answer:3,hint:"Recall the printed results: Z(LT) 1.11, Z(ST) 2.61, Cpk 0.87, Ppk 0.37.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book (pp. 224–225): 2,666 dropped calls out of 20,000 → Z(LT) 1.11 → Z(ST) 2.61 → Cpk 0.87, Ppk 0.37."},{title:"Apply",body:"That is about a 2.6 sigma process, far below the desired Cpk 1.33."}],final:"About 2.6σ, Cpk 0.87 → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the example’s results are far from six sigma.","Incorrect — the example’s Cpk is 0.87, below the desired 1.33.","Incorrect — the book shows Ppk (0.37) lower than Cpk (0.87).",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 224–225"},{id:"sv-2-1938",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which statement about the Z table is CORRECT?",options:["It lists the DPU of every possible process","It links a Z-score to the tail area of the standard normal curve beyond it","It gives the control limits for a p chart","It converts Cpk directly into Ppk"],answer:1,hint:"The table holds areas under the normal curve.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book (p. 222): the Z table links a Z-score to the **tail area** (the proportion beyond it). Forward: Z → area; reverse: area → Z."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"DPU, control limits and index conversions do not come from the table."}],final:"Z table = Z ↔ tail area → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — DPU is calculated from the data; the table holds normal-curve areas.","","Incorrect — p-chart limits are calculated from the process data.","Incorrect — Cpk and Ppk differ by the 1.5 shift in Z, not by a table look-up."],bookRef:"Book p. 222"},{id:"sv-2-1939",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which statement about a Z-score is CORRECT?",options:["It is the proportion of output beyond the specification limit","It is the number of standard deviations a value lies from the mean","It is the number of defects per unit","It is the width of the specification divided by the process spread"],answer:1,hint:"Z counts distance in a particular unit.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book (p. 222): a Z-score is the distance, in standard deviations, of a value from the mean. The Z table links it to the tail area."},{title:"Trap",body:"Do not confuse the Z-score with the proportion it corresponds to."}],final:"Z = distance in standard deviations → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — that proportion is the tail area found from the Z-score, not the Z-score itself.","","Incorrect — defects per unit is DPU.","Incorrect — specification width over process spread describes Cp."],trap:"A Z-score is a distance, not a proportion of data.",bookRef:"Book p. 222"},{id:"sv-2-1940",phase:2,topic:"2.4.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about PPM and DPMO is CORRECT?",options:["PPM and DPMO always give the same number for the same process","PPM counts defective units per million units; DPMO counts defects per million opportunities","PPM counts defects per million opportunities; DPMO counts defective units","PPM is used only for continuous data and DPMO only for attribute data"],answer:1,hint:"Check what is counted on top and what is counted below.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"PPM = proportion defective × 1,000,000 (units). DPMO = defects ÷ (units × opportunities) × 1,000,000."},{title:"Apply",body:"One counts failed units, the other counts defects per chance to fail."}],final:"PPM = units, DPMO = opportunities → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — they share a value only when each unit has one opportunity.","","Incorrect — this swaps the two definitions.","Incorrect — both are used for attribute rates; PPM also appears in continuous reports."],bookRef:"Book pp. 222–224"}];export{e as items};
